and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n540:  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n543:  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n549:  survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the\n552:  it is given is on a different logical stream, never a defect. A descriptor\n555:- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n557:  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n563:  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n576:| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n590:`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n596:| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n607:answer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n611:(`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`) declare clean failure: their\n629:`ok` is the local lifecycle transition, not a report on releasing host\n639:ownership merely by being on the serving host. An attached\n641:stream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\n646:what *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n648:closes, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n664:- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.\n668:  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.\n703:`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n712:reader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n713:deliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n714:receives a `gap`.\n716:When bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n717:gap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n718:nothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n732:**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n5:   `make-port` composes the local socket's protocol state, host-free: a\n6:   host adapter feeds every received datagram to `receive!` with its\n13:   complete decoded message `receive!` reassembles and deposits.\n35:   way a WebSocket connection's own traffic medium is not. `projection`\n36:   and `step!` are this channel's `ws-project`: driven per attachment\n41:   `make-attacher` binds or reuses `:port` and answers a writer handle\n42:   whose `append!` sends toward the descriptor's address, fragmenting\n43:   and refusing oversize per the above; `send-to!` is the same write\n47:   claim, because `receive!` is the only place a source address is\n77:(defn descriptor?\n89:(defn address-key\n202:(defn make-port\n230:(defn send-value!\n256:(defn send-to!\n366:  (stream/append! (:traffic @port)\n373:(defn receive!\n403:;; The projection: one channel ring per attachment (this channel's\n407:(defn projection\n408:  \"Compose one projection over the attachment identity `:attachment`\n412:   read. Owns the cursor from here: each `step!` advances it.\"\n419:    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp projection composition\"\n426:(defn step!\n427:  \"Drive the projection once, at the composition's cadence: read the\n431:   Returns the projection.\"\n441:            (stream/append! (:ring s) (:dao.stream.udp/value event)))\n451:(defn reading-cursor\n460:(deftype UdpHandle\n474:  (append!\n479:(defn make-attacher\n481:   an already-composed `make-port` value. `attach!` binds or reuses it\n483:   it -- and answers at once with a writer handle whose `append!` sends\nsrc/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj\nsrc/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd\nsrc/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj\nsrc/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs\nsrc/cljd/d