{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '35,82p;96,132p;330,357p;405,462p;570,625p'; nl -ba src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj | sed -n '1,175p'; nl -ba src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs | sed -n '1,160p'; nl -ba src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd | sed -n '1,160p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '35,82p;96,132p;330,357p;405,462p;570,625p'; nl -ba src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj | sed -n '1,175p'; nl -ba src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs | sed -n '1,160p'; nl -ba src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd | sed -n '1,160p'\"","aggregated_output":"    35\t\n    36\tThe design separates five concepts:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n    39\t2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n    40\t   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n    41\t3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n    42\t   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n    43\t4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n    44\t   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n    45\t5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n    46\t   logical stream.\n    47\t\n    48\tCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n    49\tidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n    50\tstate.\n    51\t\n    52\tSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n    53\tResult Convention):\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n    60\t| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n    61\t| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n    62\t| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n    63\t| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n    64\t\n    65\tA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\n    66\tdeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\n    67\tconsume no handle and produce one.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Each outcome set is exhaustive **for this version of the contract**. A\n   114\t  consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   115\t  `ok`; nothing observed; for a write, effect unknown and no automatic\n   116\t  retry; and not retryable unless the result map carries\n   117\t  `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an unrecognized\n   118\t  outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly. A\n   119\t  transport may carry `:dao.stream/retry? true` on any non-`ok` outcome to\n   120\t  say that asking again is the right response.\n   121\t- `:dao.stream/refused` is an outcome `cursor`, `next` and `append!` may\n   122\t  produce: a policy composed on this handle declined the operation; nothing\n   123\t  was observed and nothing was appended; not retryable unless the map\n   124\t  carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. `descriptor` and `close!` never produce\n   125\t  it: a name is not gated and a lifecycle transition is not declined. What\n   126\t  the policy is, and how it is composed, is outside this contract.\n   127\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   128\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   129\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   130\t  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n   131\t  and never callbacks.\n   132\t- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.\n   330\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |\n   331\t|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   332\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists, its identity settled at once. Implies not that every resource backing it has finished being acquired. | `:dao.stream/handle` |\n   333\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |\n   334\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |\n   335\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |\n   336\t\n   337\t`attach!` outcomes:\n   338\t\n   339\t| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |\n   340\t|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n   341\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle exists through which this attachment is operated. Implies neither that a far end has confirmed the stream it names nor that establishment has completed. Where either answer is one only the far end can give, it arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle`, and `:dao.stream/attachment` where the transport distinguishes attachments |\n   342\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |\n   343\t| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |\n   344\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |\n   345\t\n   346\tWhere a transport acquires its medium through a process rather than at once —\n   347\tbinding a port, opening a handle through an API that only answers later — `ok`\n   348\tstill means the logical stream exists: its identity is minted, its sequence is\n   349\tempty, and the handle is operable. What it does not mean is that the stream can\n   350\tyet accept values. The handle reports that through its own operations, exactly\n   351\tas any other transient condition is reported, and the result of the acquisition\n   352\tarrives as data.\n   353\t\n   354\tThis is permitted only where logical existence is genuinely independent of the\n   355\tresource. A transport that cannot mint identity and lifecycle before its\n   356\tacquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\n   357\tdeferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\n   405\t## Surfaces\n   406\t\n   407\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   408\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   409\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   410\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   411\t\n   412\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   413\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   414\tAn operation a handle does not\n   415\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   416\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   417\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   418\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   419\toperation exists.\n   420\t\n   421\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   422\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   423\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   424\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   425\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   426\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   427\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   428\t\n   429\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   430\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   431\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   432\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   433\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   434\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   435\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   436\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   437\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   438\t\n   439\tA handle may be composed over another handle on the same logical stream: it\n   440\tdeclares that stream's identity, implements no surface the inner handle\n   441\tlacks, passes cursors through unchanged, and may present values under its\n   442\town interpretation. Such a handle is host-composed, like a transport\n   443\tconstructor's closures, and is otherwise an ordinary handle.\n   444\t\n   445\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   446\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   447\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   448\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   449\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   450\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   451\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   452\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   453\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   454\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   455\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   456\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   457\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   458\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   459\t\n   460\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   461\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   462\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   570\t\n   571\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   572\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   573\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   574\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   575\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   576\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   577\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   578\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   579\t| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the read; nothing was observed.     | —                                                                                       |\n   580\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   581\t\n   582\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   583\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   584\tother. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no\n   585\ttake. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one\n   586\treader's progress every other reader's data loss.\n   587\t\n   588\t## Writing\n   589\t\n   590\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   591\t\n   592\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   593\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   594\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   595\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   596\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   597\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   598\t| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the append; nothing was appended.                                                                          | —             |\n   599\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport declares that its failures are clean.     | —             |\n   600\t\n   601\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   602\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   603\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   604\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   605\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   606\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   607\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   608\t\n   609\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   610\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf. The three in-memory transports\n   611\t(`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`) declare clean failure: their\n   612\t`transport-error` means nothing was appended.\n   613\t\n   614\t**Deduplication and correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must\n   615\tretry safely puts an identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice\n   616\tdeduplicates on it. The stream never promises exactly-once, and a\n   617\t`transport-error` whose effect is unknown is never retried automatically.\n   618\t\n   619\tA transport whose acceptance is not its final answer (durability reached,\n   620\tfar-end refusal) declares the channel on which that answer is deposited, as\n   621\t*Close* already requires for a failing flush.\n   622\t\n   623\t## Close\n   624\t\n   625\t`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp.jvm\n     2\t  \"The JVM host edge for dao.stream.udp: one DatagramSocket, bound once,\n     3\t   with a daemon receiver thread that feeds every datagram's source\n     4\t   address and raw bytes to a composed `receive-fn`\n     5\t   (dao.stream.udp/receive!, partially applied over the port), and the\n     6\t   :send! seam dao.stream.udp/make-port composes over. All protocol\n     7\t   logic -- fragmentation, reassembly, the deposit shape -- stays in\n     8\t   dao.stream.udp; this adapter only owns the socket and its thread.\"\n     9\t  (:import (java.net DatagramPacket DatagramSocket InetAddress)))\n    10\t\n    11\t\n    12\t(defn bind!\n    13\t  \"Bind a DatagramSocket at `port` (0 for an ephemeral port) and start\n    14\t   its daemon receiver thread. Returns {:socket s :local-port p :send!\n    15\t   f :close! f}; `:send!` is `(send! host dest-port bytes) -> nil`,\n    16\t   the port composition's host seam.\"\n    17\t  ([receive-fn] (bind! receive-fn 0))\n    18\t  ([receive-fn port]\n    19\t   (let [socket (DatagramSocket. (int port))\n    20\t         running (atom true)\n    21\t         thread\n    22\t         (Thread.\n    23\t           (fn []\n    24\t             (let [buf (byte-array 65536)\n    25\t                   packet (DatagramPacket. buf (alength buf))]\n    26\t               (while @running\n    27\t                 ;; .receive leaves the packet's length at what the last\n    28\t                 ;; datagram filled; reset it to the buffer's own length\n    29\t                 ;; so a datagram after a short one is not truncated.\n    30\t                 (.setLength packet (alength buf))\n    31\t                 (when (try (.receive socket packet)\n    32\t                            true\n    33\t                            (catch Exception _\n    34\t                              (when @running (Thread/sleep 10))\n    35\t                              false))\n    36\t                   (let [n (.getLength packet)\n    37\t                         bytes (java.util.Arrays/copyOfRange\n    38\t                                 (.getData packet) 0 n)\n    39\t                         host (.getHostAddress (.getAddress packet))\n    40\t                         src-port (.getPort packet)]\n    41\t                     (try (receive-fn host src-port bytes)\n    42\t                          (catch Exception _ nil))))))))]\n    43\t     (.setDaemon thread true)\n    44\t     (.start thread)\n    45\t     {:socket socket\n    46\t      :local-port (.getLocalPort socket)\n    47\t      :send! (fn [host dest-port bytes]\n    48\t               (.send socket\n    49\t                      (DatagramPacket. bytes (alength bytes)\n    50\t                                       (InetAddress/getByName host)\n    51\t                                       (int dest-port)))\n    52\t               nil)\n    53\t      :close! (fn []\n    54\t                (reset! running false)\n    55\t                (.close socket)\n    56\t                nil)})))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp.node\n     2\t  \"The Node host edge for dao.stream.udp: one `dgram` socket, bound\n     3\t   once, whose \\\"message\\\" event feeds every datagram's source address\n     4\t   and raw bytes to a composed `receive-fn` (dao.stream.udp/receive!,\n     5\t   partially applied over the port), and the :send! seam\n     6\t   dao.stream.udp/make-port composes over. All protocol logic --\n     7\t   fragmentation, reassembly, the deposit shape -- stays in\n     8\t   dao.stream.udp; this adapter only owns the socket. A Node Buffer\n     9\t   already satisfies dao.stream.cbor's byte-payload? (it extends\n    10\t   Uint8Array), so a received message reaches `receive-fn` unconverted.\"\n    11\t  (:require [\"dgram\" :as dgram]))\n    12\t\n    13\t\n    14\t(defn bind!\n    15\t  \"Bind a UDP socket at `port` (0 for an ephemeral port). Returns\n    16\t   {:socket s :local-port p :send! f :close! f} once bound; `on-bound`\n    17\t   is called with that map, because Node's bind is asynchronous.\"\n    18\t  ([receive-fn on-bound] (bind! receive-fn 0 on-bound))\n    19\t  ([receive-fn port on-bound]\n    20\t   (let [socket (.createSocket dgram \"udp4\")]\n    21\t     (.on socket \"message\"\n    22\t          (fn [msg rinfo]\n    23\t            (receive-fn (.-address rinfo) (.-port rinfo) msg)))\n    24\t     (.on socket \"listening\"\n    25\t          (fn []\n    26\t            (let [address (.address socket)]\n    27\t              (on-bound\n    28\t                {:socket socket\n    29\t                 :local-port (.-port address)\n    30\t                 :send! (fn [host dest-port bytes]\n    31\t                          (.send socket bytes dest-port host)\n    32\t                          nil)\n    33\t                 :close! (fn [] (.close socket) nil)}))))\n    34\t     (.bind socket port))))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp.dart\n     2\t  \"The dart:io host edge for dao.stream.udp: one RawDatagramSocket,\n     3\t   bound once, whose event stream feeds every datagram's source\n     4\t   address and raw bytes to a composed `receive-fn`\n     5\t   (dao.stream.udp/receive!, partially applied over the port), and the\n     6\t   :send! seam dao.stream.udp/make-port composes over. All protocol\n     7\t   logic -- fragmentation, reassembly, the deposit shape -- stays in\n     8\t   dao.stream.udp; this adapter only owns the socket.\"\n     9\t  (:require [\"dart:async\" :as async]\n    10\t            [\"dart:io\" :as io]))\n    11\t\n    12\t\n    13\t(defn bind!\n    14\t  \"Bind a RawDatagramSocket at `port` (0 for an ephemeral port).\n    15\t   Returns a Future of {:socket s :local-port p :send! f :close! f}.\"\n    16\t  ([receive-fn] (bind! receive-fn 0))\n    17\t  ([receive-fn port]\n    18\t   (-> ^async/Future (io/RawDatagramSocket.bind \"0.0.0.0\" port)\n    19\t       (.then\n    20\t         (fn [socket]\n    21\t           (let [socket ^io/RawDatagramSocket socket]\n    22\t             (.listen socket\n    23\t                      (fn [event]\n    24\t                        (when (= event io/RawSocketEvent.read)\n    25\t                          (when-some [^io/Datagram datagram\n    26\t                                      (.receive socket)]\n    27\t                            (receive-fn\n    28\t                              (.-address ^io/InternetAddress\n    29\t                               (.-address datagram))\n    30\t                              (.-port datagram)\n    31\t                              (.-data datagram))))))\n    32\t             {:socket socket\n    33\t              :local-port (.-port socket)\n    34\t              :send! (fn [host dest-port bytes]\n    35\t                       (.send socket bytes (io/InternetAddress. host)\n    36\t                              dest-port)\n    37\t                       nil)\n    38\t              :close! (fn [] (.close socket) nil)}))))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The main compatibility issue is the synchronous `dao.jing/get` handle: a remote fetch is asynchronous on Node and Dart. I’m separating local store acknowledgement from network completion so the proposed stream API does not promise a synchronous remote result it cannot provide."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc | sed -n '1,120p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '570,625p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '365,405p;455,497p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc | sed -n '227,279p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc | sed -n '220,255p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '460,475p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc | sed -n '1,120p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '570,625p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '365,405p;455,497p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc | sed -n '227,279p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc | sed -n '220,255p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '460,475p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.jing.content\n     2\t  \"Content lookup as a request and response service over remote streams\n     3\t   (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md, section 5; the successor of the\n     4\t   deleted dao.jing.remote). One payload convention, spoken by every half\n     5\t   of the service and by the linker's own client:\n     6\t\n     7\t     {:jing/request r :jing/get address}\n     8\t       answered by {:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64};\n     9\t     {:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}\n    10\t       answered by {:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}.\n    11\t\n    12\t   r is a self-minted random value, so callers sharing one answers stream\n    13\t   do not collide. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text in the\n    14\t   application value; message boundaries are the channel codec's, one\n    15\t   application value per message, so the vocabulary rides Transit-JSON\n    16\t   text or canonical CBOR alike.\n    17\t\n    18\t   This namespace is the serving half: serve-step, the interpreter over a\n    19\t   local requests reader and answers writer against a dao.jing handle --\n    20\t   the Linda shape, one peer's table entry pair and its policy. The\n    21\t   client half is dao.jing.content.step; the JVM blocking driver is\n    22\t   dao.jing.content.driver; the callback facade is\n    23\t   dao.jing.content.async. Every put passes through the one ingress\n    24\t   canonicality check, dao.jing/accept-bytes!.\"\n    25\t  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n    26\t            [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    27\t\n    28\t\n    29\t(def get-op\n    30\t  \"The request key of a content read.\"\n    31\t  :jing/get)\n    32\t\n    33\t\n    34\t(def put-op\n    35\t  \"The request key of a content write.\"\n    36\t  :jing/put)\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t(defn- valid-handle!\n    40\t  \"Throw unless `handle` exposes the byte-store effects the interpreter\n    41\t   calls; the error is a composition defect, not a wire event.\"\n    42\t  [handle]\n    43\t  (when-not (ifn? (:put-bytes-fn handle))\n    44\t    (throw (ex-info \"a content handle must expose a :put-bytes-fn\"\n    45\t                    {:handle handle})))\n    46\t  (when-not (ifn? (:get-bytes-fn handle))\n    47\t    (throw (ex-info \"a content handle must expose a :get-bytes-fn\"\n    48\t                    {:handle handle})))\n    49\t  handle)\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\t(defn- get-request?\n    53\t  \"True only for the exact read request {:jing/request r :jing/get a}.\"\n    54\t  [v]\n    55\t  (and (map? v) (= #{:jing/request :jing/get} (set (keys v)))))\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(defn- put-request?\n    59\t  \"True only for the exact write request {:jing/request r :jing/put a\n    60\t   :jing/bytes b64}.\"\n    61\t  [v]\n    62\t  (and (map? v)\n    63\t       (= #{:jing/request :jing/put :jing/bytes} (set (keys v)))))\n    64\t\n    65\t\n    66\t(defn- append-answer!\n    67\t  \"Deliver one answer value; the writer's refusal loses that one answer,\n    68\t   exactly as any transport loses a message -- the caller's own retry or\n    69\t   timeout policy owns the loss, never the interpreter.\"\n    70\t  [answers value]\n    71\t  (stream/append! answers value)\n    72\t  nil)\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\t(defn- serve-one!\n    76\t  \"Answer one well-formed request, or drop it as malformed wire input:\n    77\t   a value that is neither an exact read nor an exact write, a read of a\n    78\t   non-address, and a write whose bytes fail the ingress check are all\n    79\t   dropped -- no answer, the cursor advances past them, and the caller's\n    80\t   outstanding request owns the silence. A backend verdict outside\n    81\t   #{:inserted :present} is a composition defect and throws here, before\n    82\t   the cursor moves, so the same request is re-read once the backend is\n    83\t   fixed (the observer's effect rule, docs/design/dao.jing.md).\"\n    84\t  [handle answers v]\n    85\t  (let [r (get v :jing/request)\n    86\t        missing #?(:clj (Object.) :cljs (js-obj) :cljd (Object.))]\n    87\t    (cond\n    88\t      (and (get-request? v) (jing/segment-address? (get v get-op)))\n    89\t      (let [address (get v get-op)\n    90\t            result ((:get-bytes-fn handle) address missing)]\n    91\t        (if (identical? result missing)\n    92\t          (append-answer! answers {:jing/request r, :jing/found? false,\n    93\t                                   :jing/bytes nil})\n    94\t          (append-answer! answers {:jing/request r, :jing/found? true,\n    95\t                                   :jing/bytes (jing/bytes->base64\n    96\t                                                 result)})))\n    97\t\n    98\t      (and (put-request? v) (jing/segment-address? (get v put-op)))\n    99\t      (let [address (get v put-op)\n   100\t            verdict (if-some [bs (try (jing/accept-bytes!\n   101\t                                        address (get v :jing/bytes))\n   102\t                                      (catch #?(:cljd Object\n   103\t                                                :clj Throwable\n   104\t                                                :cljs :default)\n   105\t                                             _\n   106\t                                        nil))]\n   107\t                      ((:put-bytes-fn handle) address bs)\n   108\t                      ::refused)]\n   109\t        (when-not (= ::refused verdict)\n   110\t          (when-not (#{:inserted :present} verdict)\n   111\t            (throw (ex-info \"backend returned an invalid put result\"\n   112\t                            {:address address, :result verdict})))\n   113\t          (append-answer! answers {:jing/request r, :jing/result verdict})))\n   114\t\n   115\t      ;; anything else is malformed wire input, dropped below the step\n   116\t      :else nil)))\n   117\t\n   118\t\n   119\t(defn serve-step\n   120\t  \"One non-waiting advance of the content interpreter: answer at most\n   570\t- **Garbage collection.** Content reachability and reclamation belong to a\n   571\t  higher-level retention policy; immutable content otherwise accumulates\n   572\t  forever.\n   573\t- **Async hydration.** Readers of remote or async B-tree content use the\n   574\t  hydration adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage` and\n   575\t  `hydrate!`), but the async variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`)\n   576\t  are deferred until an async DaoJing backend exists. See\n   577\t  `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4.\n   578\t\n   579\t  The remote half of that deferral is the **stepped client**: a\n   580\t  non-blocking remote handle with the `request-put` / `request-get` /\n   581\t  `request-materialize` / `step` / `abandon` shape — a client the caller\n   582\t  steps on a host that cannot wait. It is owed to the async hydration\n   583\t  work (`docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4), not to the v1 deletion: it\n   584\t  needs multi-id dispatch and per-materialization records rather than the\n   585\t  blocking driver's per-id step, and it forces a consumer change on\n   586\t  B-tree hydration.\n   587\t\n   588\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (stepped client):** the client itself is built —\n   589\t  `dao.jing.remote.step` wraps one `dao.stream.rpc` client state with\n   590\t  per-materialization records and id routes, `step` advancing in the\n   591\t  recorded fixed order (retry unsent, poll, terminal-abandon, issue\n   592\t  verify reads in put-id order, drain-and-route), the `:present` verify\n   593\t  hop hashing the read-back against the address exactly as\n   594\t  `materialize!` does, one published completion per materialization\n   595\t  carrying its put id, and no payload retained or published anywhere.\n   596\t  What remains owed to `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 is the consumer side:\n   597\t  an async backend over this client, `hydrate-async`, and\n   598\t  `store-tree-async`.\n   599\t\n   600\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (consumer side):** built.\n   601\t  `dao.jing.remote.async/async-content` is the async backend: it owns one\n   602\t  stepped state as its single step owner, queues requests from any\n   603\t  caller, and drives `step` with a self-rescheduling pump (setTimeout /\n   604\t  Dart `Timer` / the JVM delayed executor, or an injected `:schedule`),\n   605\t  answering each request's callback exactly once with its published\n   606\t  completion. `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async` and\n   607\t  `store-tree-async` consume it through a `hydration-storage` whose\n   608\t  source is that handle; see `dao.data.btree.md` §6 Phase 4 notes.\n   609\t\n   610\t  **Status 2026-09-27:** `dao.jing.remote.step` and `dao.jing.remote.async`\n   611\t  are deprecated with `dao.jing.remote`. `dao.stream.remote.md` section 8\n   612\t  names their successors, `dao.jing.content.step` and\n   613\t  `dao.jing.content.async`, with the same shapes, whose media are any\n   614\t  `dao.stream` handles: reflections of remote streams or local ring\n   615\t  buffers.\n   616\t\n   617\t## Lineage\n   618\t\n   619\tDaoJing combines two constraints:\n   620\t\n   621\t- **Datomic:** storage retains immutable content while an embeddable reader\n   622\t  interprets indexes and queries above it.\n   623\t- **Plan 9:** location and transport are properties of streams and handles,\n   624\t  not of the values carried through them.\n   625\t\n   365\t  [port attachment host src-port v]\n   366\t  (stream/append! (:traffic @port)\n   367\t                  {:dao.stream.udp/attachment attachment\n   368\t                   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host host\n   369\t                                           :dao.stream.udp/port src-port}\n   370\t                   :dao.stream.udp/value v}))\n   371\t\n   372\t\n   373\t(defn receive!\n   374\t  \"Adapter entry: one raw datagram arrived from `host`:`src-port` on\n   375\t   `port`'s socket, as `bytes`. Malformed CBOR is dropped as a\n   376\t   diagnostic below the mirror and the link, per dao.stream.remote.md\n   377\t   2.1, and so is a fragment envelope whose own fields are malformed\n   378\t   -- checked before any partial state can change. A plain value\n   379\t   deposits at once; a fragment envelope folds into reassembly and\n   380\t   deposits only once complete -- loss of any part is loss of the\n   381\t   whole message, recovered by the link's resend rule, not by this\n   382\t   port. Replies to what this call learns go to `host`:`src-port`,\n   383\t   this datagram's own source, never an address a payload might\n   384\t   claim.\"\n   385\t  [port host src-port bytes]\n   386\t  (when-some [v (try (cbor/decode bytes) (catch #?(:cljd Object\n   387\t                                                   :clj Throwable\n   388\t                                                   :cljs :default) _\n   389\t                                           nil))]\n   390\t    (let [attachment (address-key host src-port)]\n   391\t      (if (fragment-envelope? v)\n   392\t        (when (well-formed-fragment? v)\n   393\t          (when-some [payload (absorb-fragment! port attachment v)]\n   394\t            (when-some [whole (try (cbor/decode payload)\n   395\t                                   (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable\n   396\t                                             :cljs :default) _\n   397\t                                     nil))]\n   398\t              (deposit! port attachment host src-port whole))))\n   399\t        (deposit! port attachment host src-port v)))))\n   400\t\n   401\t\n   402\t;; =============================================================================\n   403\t;; The projection: one channel ring per attachment (this channel's\n   404\t;; ws-project)\n   405\t;; =============================================================================\n   455\t\n   456\t;; =============================================================================\n   457\t;; The writer handle and attacher\n   458\t;; =============================================================================\n   459\t\n   460\t(deftype UdpHandle\n   461\t  [port host dest-port descriptor]\n   462\t\n   463\t  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n   464\t\n   465\t  (descriptor\n   466\t    [_]\n   467\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   468\t     :dao.stream/descriptor descriptor\n   469\t     :dao.stream/identity (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)})\n   470\t\n   471\t\n   472\t  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n   473\t\n   474\t  (append!\n   475\t    [_ v]\n   476\t    (send-value! port host dest-port v)))\n   477\t\n   478\t\n   479\t(defn make-attacher\n   480\t  \"Construct the host-composed unary `attach!` entry point: `:port` is\n   481\t   an already-composed `make-port` value. `attach!` binds or reuses it\n   482\t   -- one port serves every descriptor a composition attaches through\n   483\t   it -- and answers at once with a writer handle whose `append!` sends\n   484\t   toward the descriptor's address.\"\n   485\t  [{:keys [port] :as config}]\n   486\t  (when-not (some? port)\n   487\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.udp attacher needs :port\" {:config config})))\n   488\t  (fn attach!\n   489\t    [descriptor]\n   490\t    (if-not (descriptor? descriptor)\n   491\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}\n   492\t      (let [host (:dao.stream.udp/host descriptor)\n   493\t            dest-port (:dao.stream.udp/port descriptor)]\n   494\t        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   495\t         :dao.stream/handle (UdpHandle. port host dest-port descriptor)\n   496\t         :dao.stream/attachment (address-key host dest-port)}))))\n   227\t(defn- make-get\n   228\t  [{:keys [net local], :as handle}]\n   229\t  (fn [address not-found]\n   230\t    (ensure-open handle)\n   231\t    (let [v ((:get-bytes-fn local) address content-missing)]\n   232\t      (if (not (identical? v content-missing))\n   233\t        v\n   234\t        (let [self-id (:id (self-peer net))\n   235\t              fetched\n   236\t              (some (fn [peer]\n   237\t                      (when (not= self-id (:id peer))\n   238\t                        (when-let [res (safe-fetch-content net peer address)]\n   239\t                          (when (:found? res)\n   240\t                            (when-let [bs (accept-bytes address (:value res))]\n   241\t                              [bs])))))\n   242\t                    (lookup net (content-target address)))]\n   243\t          (if fetched\n   244\t            (let [bs (first fetched)\n   245\t                  result ((:put-bytes-fn local) address bs)]\n   246\t              (when-not (#{:inserted :present} result)\n   247\t                (throw\n   248\t                  (ex-info\n   249\t                    \"dao.jing.dht: fetched content could not be cached\"\n   250\t                    {:address address, :result result})))\n   251\t              bs)\n   252\t            not-found))))))\n   253\t\n   254\t\n   255\t(defn- make-close\n   256\t  [{:keys [net local closed-atom]}]\n   257\t  (fn []\n   258\t    (with-lock closed-atom\n   259\t      (fn []\n   260\t        (when-not @closed-atom\n   261\t          (let [err-net (try (close-net! net)\n   262\t                             nil\n   263\t                             (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   264\t                                       :cljs :default\n   265\t                                       :cljd Object)\n   266\t                                    e\n   267\t                               e))\n   268\t                err-local (try (jing/close! local)\n   269\t                               nil\n   270\t                               (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   271\t                                         :cljs :default\n   272\t                                         :cljd Object)\n   273\t                                      e\n   274\t                                 e))]\n   275\t            (if (or err-net err-local)\n   276\t              (throw (or err-net err-local))\n   277\t              (reset! closed-atom true))))\n   278\t        nil))))\n   279\t\n   220\t   payloads it materialized and, when the drain met a gap or a defect, what\n   221\t   it met.  The pool is not kept: its intake is dropped with the round.\"\n   222\t  [content-store pool]\n   223\t  (loop [pool pool\n   224\t         n 0]\n   225\t    (let [{:keys [signal], :as r} (jing/observe-step! content-store pool)]\n   226\t      (case signal\n   227\t        :dao.stream/ok (recur (:state r) (inc n))\n   228\t        :dao.stream/blocked [n nil]\n   229\t        [n signal]))))\n   230\t\n   231\t\n   232\t(defn- publish\n   233\t  \"Publish the covered indexes over every committed transaction through\n   234\t   the round's transactor, drain its intake into the store, and read the\n   235\t   manifest back from it.  Only a publication whose manifest the store\n   236\t   answers becomes the reported one, covering `:published` transactions.\"\n   237\t  [ix round]\n   238\t  (try\n   239\t    (let [{:keys [transactor pool]} (:publication ix)\n   240\t          {:keys [manifest-address]} (transactor/publish! transactor\n   241\t                                                          (:publish-opts ix))\n   242\t          [n defect] (materialize (:content-store ix) pool)]\n   243\t      (if defect\n   244\t        (assoc ix :failure {:round round :stage :materialize :outcome defect})\n   245\t        (do (index/read-manifest (:content-store ix) manifest-address)\n   246\t            (assoc ix\n   247\t                   :manifest-address manifest-address\n   248\t                   :published (:transactions ix)\n   249\t                   :published-payloads n))))\n   250\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) e\n   251\t      (assoc ix\n   252\t             :failure {:round round :stage :publish :message (ex-message e)}))))\n   253\t\n   254\t\n   255\t(defn- consume\n   460\tover externally supplied bytes.\n   461\t\n   462\t### Remote and DHT\n   463\t\n   464\tCarry canonical payload bytes as padded standard-alphabet Base64 strings\n   465\t(no whitespace or URL-safe alphabet) in the application value, one\n   466\tapplication value per channel-codec message: `dao.jing.content` rides a\n   467\t`dao.stream.remote.md` channel (Transit-JSON text or canonical CBOR\n   468\tbinary per WebSocket message, canonical CBOR per datagram on UDP), while\n   469\t`dao.jing.dht` keeps its own one-Transit-JSON-value-per-datagram envelope.\n   470\tEncode and decode this transport representation at\n   471\tJing's network boundaries; leave general DaoStream Transit unchanged.\n   472\tRemote and DHT APIs above the byte-store wrapper remain value-facing.\n   473\t\n   474\tDecode Base64 strictly, hash-verify received bytes against the claimed\n   475\taddress, and perform the one ingress canonicality check before accepting or\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-30 09:07:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 16:07:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n## R1. Raw datagram layer\n\n**I change my prior D1 position.** The owner has chosen a raw datagram boundary, so the DHT must not consume `dao.stream.udp`’s decoded, fragmented value channel.\n\nAdd `dao.stream.udp.raw` beneath the existing `dao.stream.udp`. A bound raw port exposes a `dao.stream` **writer handle**. Its `append!` value is plain data:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.stream.udp.raw/to    {:host \"198.51.100.7\" :port 4040}\n :dao.stream.udp.raw/bytes \"<padded standard Base64>\"}\n```\n\nBase64 represents the exact datagram bytes across CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD without placing host byte-array types in stream values. The raw layer decodes it, checks destination and a **1200-byte decoded datagram limit**, and makes one addressed send. Malformed address, Base64, or oversize yields `:dao.stream/invalid-value`; a closed port yields `:dao.stream/closed`; socket failure yields `:dao.stream/transport-error`. `:dao.stream/ok` means local send acceptance, never delivery, as the stream writing contract requires ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:588)). No raw-layer retry, ordering, reassembly, or content decoding occurs.\n\nThe socket adapter deposits each received datagram onto a caller-supplied traffic stream as `{:dao.stream.udp.raw/source {:host h :port p}, :dao.stream.udp.raw/bytes b64}`. It copies bytes before deposit and takes the source from the socket, not the payload. The socket itself has **no reader surface** because it retains nothing; consumers hold cursors on the supplied traffic stream. A bounded ring may report `:dao.stream/gap`, which each DHT or value-channel interpreter must treat as lost datagrams. This follows the explicit boundary and retention rules ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:405), [dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:570)). The raw writer’s descriptor names that bound port and its identity; its destination remains in each append value. Binding is a host-composed creation operation, and closing closes the port. Do not imply that attaching to a remote port grants a remote reader surface.\n\nRefactor the existing `dao.stream.udp` value channel to read raw traffic with an owned cursor and send encoded fragment datagrams through the raw writer. Its public value-channel descriptor, `send-to!`, projection, and `dao.stream.remote` behavior stay the same. Today `dao.stream.udp/receive!` accepts raw bytes directly and `make-port` takes a host `:send!` function ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:202), [udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:373)); those become composition internals. Existing JVM, Node, and Dart socket seams already receive source addresses and raw bytes ([jvm.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj:12), [node.cljs](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/dao/stream/udp/node.cljs:14), [dart.cljd](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd:13)). Their host callbacks may only deposit an event and return, per the host-boundary invariant ([datom.world.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:69)).\n\n## R2. DHT directly on raw datagrams\n\n**I also change my prior D2 position:** canonical CBOR is no longer forced by the value channel. Keep the present Transit-JSON encoding for small DHT RPC maps in the first migration, and version the DHT wire before adding chunk messages. The node currently sends one Transit-JSON map per datagram ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:1), [node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:57)). A later CBOR change needs its own interop reason and versioned fleet plan; content payloads remain canonical CBOR regardless of the DHT envelope ([dao.jing.cbor.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:462)).\n\nThe DHT interpreter reads raw traffic, decodes only DHT wire values, and emits replies by appending addressed raw datagrams. Small `ping`, `find-node`, `store-content`, `fetch-content`, and reply messages retain RPC IDs. For messages exceeding one datagram, define a **DHT-owned chunk protocol** over raw datagrams: version, sender request ID, request/reply direction, part index, total parts, and chunk bytes. Correlate partials by observed source address and port plus those fields. A shared, transport-neutral fragmentation *utility* is acceptable, but it must not import `dao.stream.udp`’s private envelope or its `dao.stream.remote/id` meaning ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151)). Bound encoded message size, number and bytes of partials per source and globally, and number of parts. Refuse oversize before the first send; duplicate or inconsistent parts do not grow state. Evict incomplete partials under a declared policy; retry the complete RPC after timeout. Verify the complete content against its segment address before storing or caching, as the current DHT does ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:167)).\n\nHardening belongs to the DHT **at this raw boundary**. A small `ping` can obtain a time-limited cookie bound to the observed source IP and port. Require it before a fragmented reply or allocating substantial incoming reassembly state; put a verifiable token on each incoming chunk so an attacker cannot force assembly before validation. Cap unverified replies to the request size or a small fixed budget. Match RPC replies to the expected observed source, limit work and storage per source and globally, and do not add a claimed `:from` to routing before validation. The current receiver observes `:from` before processing and matches replies only by RPC ID ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:171)). These rules must be specified with chunking; non-loopback exposure waits for their implementation.\n\n## R3. DHT’s stream-shaped API and Jing adapter\n\nExpose two composition-supplied streams: a **request writer** and an **outcome reader**. Request values carry a caller-minted correlation ID and one operation:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :put\n :dao.jing.dht/address address :dao.jing.dht/bytes b64}\n{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :get\n :dao.jing.dht/address address}\n{:dao.jing.dht/id id :dao.jing.dht/op :find\n :dao.jing.dht/target target-id}\n```\n\nThe caller’s `append!` outcome says only that the request stream accepted the value. A caller-stepped DHT interpreter reads requests and raw traffic, owns explicit pending-RPC and cursor state, and appends exactly one terminal outcome per accepted request ID: `:stored-locally` with `:inserted|:present`, `:found` with verified bytes, `:not-found`, `:refused`, `:timeout`, or `:error`. Replication progress is a separate event stream keyed by address; it cannot retroactively change the local put verdict. Results use DHT-qualified *values*, while stream operations continue to use the exhaustive `:dao.stream/outcome` vocabulary ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:96)). Duplicate request IDs and completion retention need explicit bounded policy; no callbacks or hidden worker are required.\n\nKeep the existing synchronous Jing byte-store handle as a **local compatibility adapter**. Its `put` validates and writes to the local backend, then emits an address-only replication intent; `:inserted`/`:present` means the local backend verdict. Its `get` answers local verified content immediately. A remote miss is requested and hydrated through the DHT streams, then the caller retries the local read. A synchronous, portable `get` cannot wait for a future network answer: DaoStream explicitly says operations do not wait ([dao.stream.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:69)). The current DHT handle *does* synchronously fetch on a miss ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:227)); that behavior must be isolated as a JVM blocking facade, or callers must migrate to staged hydration. Do not silently return `not-found` for an outstanding remote fetch. `yin.repl.index` can continue its synchronous local write and manifest readback ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:232)); remote query needs a hydration step before `q`. The repository already distinguishes a stepped content client from its JVM blocking driver ([content.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:18)).\n\nThe prior D3, D4, D7, D8, and D9 decisions stand: volatile best-effort replication intent with an honest local acknowledgement; a solo net with no socket; latest-root discovery outside this DHT epic, with local HEAD for owner restart; lookup and resource-bound repairs here; `mem` default and explicit network/publication choices. The raw-layer decision changes none of those contracts.\n\n## R4. Revised slices\n\n| Slice | Work | Acceptance |\n|---|---|---|\n| **S0: Contract freeze** | Specify raw values/descriptors, DHT request/result values, wire version, chunk and abuse budgets, local-versus-remote Jing behavior. | The design states every `append!` outcome and every asynchronous completion; no claim equates send acceptance with delivery. |\n| **S1: Raw datagram stream** | Build `dao.stream.udp.raw` over the existing host seams; make the value channel an interpreter above it. | JVM, Node, and Dart raw streams round-trip exact binary datagrams and observed source addresses; oversize is `invalid-value`; loss and ring gaps are observable; existing value-channel and `dao.stream.remote` tests still pass. |\n| **S2: DHT stream core and solo mode** | Request/outcome streams, caller-stepped interpreter, local Jing adapter, volatile address-only replication intent. | Put has no lookup or peer wait; outcomes correlate under concurrent requests; solo mode opens no socket or replication queue; local file-backed acknowledgements survive restart. |\n| **S3: DHT raw wire and bounded chunk transfer** | Connect DHT interpreter directly to raw traffic/writer; add DHT-owned chunking and whole-content verification. | Multi-node transfer of index-sized segments succeeds with reordering/loss and explicit retries; oversize and malformed chunks stay within all declared bounds. Only loopback exposure is allowed at this stage. |\n| **S4: Hardening and lookup repair** | Cookies, source matching, rate/resource limits, dead-peer replacement. | Spoofed requests cannot trigger fragmented amplification or substantial reassembly; a live next-nearest peer remains fetchable when closer peers fail. |\n| **S5: REPL integration** | After the durable-store epic supplies store-spec, HEAD, locking, and recovery, add explicit DHT choice, public-data gate, and remote hydration/query entry point requiring a supplied manifest address. | Local-only restart works without a socket; separate processes use separate locked directories; remote query works from a supplied root; source/index content is neither published nor served without explicit public opt-in. |\n\nS1 can run alongside S2 **only after S0**, with separate file and test ownership; S3 depends on both. S4’s design is part of S0, and its implementation follows S3 before exposure. S5 is externally dependent on the durable-store epic. No earlier slice touches `yin/repl/*`.\n\n## R5. Items for the owner\n\n1. **Confirm the publication policy:** whether explicit public-index opt-in may publish REPL code before ShiBi, or whether all REPL code publication is prohibited until ShiBi. Either choice must be enforced at both origin and serving paths.\n2. **Confirm the remote-read scope:** local HEAD covers restart, while a reader given only a peer address still cannot discover the latest manifest. Automatic latest-root discovery would require a separate root-fact design or an added epic slice.\n3. **Choose the synchronous compatibility boundary:** permit a JVM-only blocking DHT `get` facade, or require all remote reads to use staged hydration. The stream-shaped API itself remains nonblocking on every host.\n\nThe owner’s raw-layer decision settles the previous D1/D2 transport dependency: the DHT owns its datagram protocol, while both the raw port and the DHT expose `dao.stream` interfaces."}}
